WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.729
[MUSIC]

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.432
Product strategy conversations
are happening without marketing executives

00:00:04.432 --> 00:00:05.070
present.

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:09.820
And marketing which is really the place in
the firm where the rubber meets the road

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.230
in the sense of where
the firm meets the customer.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:15.358
For marketing not to have a voice in those
conversations doesn't make any sense.

00:00:15.358 --> 00:00:19.282
[MUSIC]

00:00:19.282 --> 00:00:23.322
The Emerging CMO Strategic Marketing
Leadership program is a program

00:00:23.322 --> 00:00:26.270
intended to bring marketing
back to the table.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:30.340
Sure companies has CMOs, but
those CMOs don't participate

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.670
necessarily in the key strategic
decisions of the firm.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:38.410
What we are looking to do with this
program is to retool emerging marketing

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:42.370
leaders in order to be able to serve as
that strategic marketing voice that's so

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.250
critical for the success of a firm.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.080
&gt;&gt; The CEO is looking at
the CMO to provide input so

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.370
that if as the organization
begins to transform,

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.630
those inputs become relevant
because at the end of the day for

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.440
an organization to be relevant,
they have to be relevant for the customer.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.350
&gt;&gt; Our programme has three
essential components.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.920
The first component is strategic, how do
we think about the firm's strategy and

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.800
how marketing fits within it?

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.980
The second component has to do with
the customer, and here we have

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:13.010
an enormous depth of expertise at Stanford
on the psychology of the customer.

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:14.800
What is it that drives
customer experiences?

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.570
What drives customer decision making?

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:20.680
How do emotions fit into how customers
decide, both in B2C settings and

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:21.260
in B2B settings.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.740
And the last component is leadership.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.640
Leading is not just a matter
of having good ideas but

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.020
it's in being able to package
those ideas so people follow you.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:34.020
You're experiencing this curated
content by some of the very

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.560
top faculty that the Stanford Graduate
School in Business has to offer,

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.690
really world leaders in their field.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.840
The goal of this program is not simply
to give you tools on strategy and

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.020
understanding customers and
consumer behavior, but

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:50.330
also almost equally importantly to
give you the tools to then carry those

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.930
frameworks back into your company.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:55.990
What we want is for our participants
to walk out of this week and say,

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:57.670
I got some great ideas about strategy.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:00.290
The second thing is I really got

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:03.750
in depth understanding of
the psychology of the customer.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.270
How is it that people out there
are thinking about their products?

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:08.050
How is it that they're
thinking about the market?

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:09.590
How are they thinking
about the competition?

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.390
How do we reach them?

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:15.568
Have the tools to be able to then carry
that story forward within an organization.

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:20.874
[MUSIC]

